  "title": "# Dev Console as an Android app: wrapper, one codebase (Brett, Nov 7) Brett chose the wrapper over …",
  "asked": "# Dev Console as an Android app: wrapper, one codebase (Brett, Nov 7) Brett chose the wrapper over a native rewrite: the existing web console runs inside an Android app shell, same way AmberX loads its web plugins. One codebase; every console change shows on the phone at once. Reuse: orchestrator build `console-android-app-tabs` (19 steps, pending) — get its brief (`node apps/console-web/plugins/build-orchestrator/orchestrator.mjs brief console-android-app-tabs`) and use what fits; ignore the archived native rewrite `console-android-native-tabs`. Reuse AmberX's Android shell/bridge pieces (apps/amberx-droid) rather than writing new ones. 1. Android app shell loading the console (console.amberxai.com), with sign-in kept, back button/navigation, pull-to-refresh, offline screen. 2. Native bridges where needed: push notifications (build start/finish/fail/land alerts), mic for voice, background wake, file share/download (APKs), deep links that open a console screen. 3. Console UI stays asset-driven; phone layouts come from the existing three-layouts/phone assets, no hard-coded styling. 4. APK built in CI next to amberx-debug.apk; listed under Apps in the console/axHub for install. 5. UI Review with three options (--choices) for the shell chrome. Tests. Add to Shipped features. Progress: append `<time> START|DONE <step>` to PROGRESS.md here at each step; don't stop between steps. Commit, don't push. Watch live: Brett can watch the screen you are changing hot-render in the console. Whenever you start work on a screen or element, run `node framework/cli/axf.mjs focus <app> [route] [css-selector] --note \"<what you are changing>\"` (apps: console-web, axhub-web, amberx-droid, walkthrough-studio, demo, framework, console, axhub-android, voicevault). Agent cap: heavy steps (pnpm/npm/vite builds, docker node:22 test runs, Playwright) share a VPS-wide lock. Run each one as `node /opt/axf/deploy/../framework/cli/agent-slots.mjs heavy -- <command>` so only a few run at once. Model switches: a downgrade (e.g. Fable → Opus) is fine. Fable upgrades need no approval right now (Brett, Nov 4 2026: \"If it needs Fable, set it to be Fable\"): a step that needs Fable (schemas, asset types, contracts, engines/kernel, cross-app protocols) is set to Fable and runs on Fable; the switch is recorded on the build. Never launch a step on a higher model than it is pinned to: raise the step first.",
